{"id": 15301, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">R</mark>esearchers studying chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) observe that in some patients the disease is indolent, with limited growth over more than a decade, whereas in other patients the disease is more aggressive and becomes symptomatic early on.\u00a0 They find that genetic abnormalities can be detected in most patients with CLL and may play a role in determining the difference between the 2 courses of the disease.\u00a0 A clinical study is conducted with the hypothesis that individuals with CLL whose leukemic cells contain trisomy of chromosome 12 have a more favorable course than those whose leukemic cells contain other genetic changes.\u00a0 Which of the following laboratory techniques would be most useful for identifying patients with this genetic abnormality?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Flow cytometry"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Gene sequencing"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Northern blotting"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Targeted mutation analysis"}], "correctAnswer": "B", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_T101541\" style=\"overflow-x: auto; font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark>pplications of common genetic tests</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Test</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Applications</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Limitations</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Gene sequencing</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Detects mutations within a single gene (eg, point mutation)</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cannot detect whole gene deletion</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Fluorescence in situ hybridization</strong>(FISH)</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Identifies targeted chromosomal abnormalities (eg, deletion, duplication, trisomy)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Specific probe(s) used for a suspected diagnosis (eg, <em style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">BCR-ABL</em> fusion in CML)</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cannot detect chromosomal abnormalities other than those specified by labeled probe</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Chromosomalmicroarray</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Identifies targeted chromosomal abnormalities (eg, microdeletion, microduplication)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Many probes allow for simultaneous assessment of multiple abnormalities for a broad diagnosis (eg, autism)</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cannot detect chromosomal abnormalities other than those specified by labeled probe</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cannot detect balanced translocations</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Karyotype</strong>(cytogenetic analysis)</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Detects large chromosomal abnormalities (eg, aneuploidy)</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Cannot detect small chromosomal abnormalities</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">CML</strong> = chronic myeloid leukemia.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Cytogenetic analysis of malignant cells often reveals characteristic chromosomal abnormalities (eg, trisomy) that deregulate gene expression.\u00a0 Identification of these abnormalities can aid in diagnosis, treatment selection, and estimation of prognosis.\u00a0 In this study, the most useful cytogenetic test to identify leukemic cells with trisomy 12 is <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">fluorescence in situ hybridization</strong> (FISH).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">FISH is a sensitive and specific targeted cytogenetic test in which fluorescently labeled probes are used to <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">identify known chromosomal abnormalities</strong> (eg, specific duplications, deletions, or translocations).\u00a0 The probes are DNA segments (often tens to hundreds of kilobases) complementary to the sequence of interest (eg, centromeric region of chromosome 12).\u00a0 After denaturation, the probes hybridize to complementary chromosomal DNA, allowing the target sequence to be identified visually and quantitatively.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Like any cytogenetic test, FISH has advantages and disadvantages.\u00a0 It has <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">higher resolution than</strong> traditional <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">karyotyping</strong> (ie, it can detect submicroscopic abnormalities) and can be performed on dividing (metaphase) and nondividing (<strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">interphase</strong>) cells.\u00a0 However, FISH detects only abnormalities involving the specific regions interrogated; <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">small insertions/deletions</strong> may be missed depending on probe design and resolution.\u00a0 In addition, standard FISH assays generally assess the presence and location of a target sequence rather than its orientation; therefore, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">inversions</strong> are often <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">missed</strong> unless probes are designed to span or flank the inversion breakpoints.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Although flow cytometry is often used in the diagnosis of leukemia, it identifies characteristic surface markers (eg, CD5) on malignant cells, not chromosomal duplications.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choices C and E)</strong>\u00a0 Gene sequencing can detect an unknown mutation in a single gene whereas targeted mutation analysis can identify a known mutation via use of a labeled probe.\u00a0 Both techniques evaluate for only the presence or absence of a mutation (not quantitative abnormalities) and are unable to detect chromosomal duplications.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice D)</strong>\u00a0 Northern blotting is a technique that size-fractionates RNA and then targets a specific mRNA sequence of interest.\u00a0 It does not identify DNA and would not detect chromosomal duplications.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Fluorescence in situ hybridization is a highly sensitive cytogenetic test that can identify known chromosomal abnormalities (eg, duplication, deletion) using a fluorescent DNA probe that is often tens to hundreds of kilobases in length and complementary to the region of interest.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization is a highly sensitive cytogenetic test that can identify known chromosomal abnormalities (eg, duplication, deletion) using a fluorescent DNA probe that is often tens to hundreds of kilobases in length and complementary to the region of interest.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c", "subject": "Biochemistry", "system": "Hematology & Oncology", "topic": "Laboratory techniques", "category": "White blood cell disorders", "peerCorrect": 43.0}